@startuml
skinparam monochrome true
skinparam defaultFontSize 10

title Power Control Register (Offset 029h)

rectangle "Register Bit Layout" {
    rectangle "D07-D04\nRsvd\n(Reserved)" as D07
    rectangle "D03-D01\nSD Bus Voltage\nSelect" as D03
    rectangle "D00\nSD Bus\nPower" as D00
}

D07 -right-> D03
D03 -right-> D00

rectangle "Register Fields" {
    rectangle "Bits 07-04: Reserved (Rsvd)" as F1
    
    rectangle "Bits 03-01: SD Bus Voltage Select (RW)\n\n111b = 3.3V (Typ.)\n110b = 3.0V (Typ.)\n101b = 1.8V (Typ.)\n100b-000b = Reserved\n\nHost Driver selects voltage level for SD card.\nCheck Voltage Support bits in Capabilities register.\nIf unsupported voltage selected, Host System\nshall not supply SD Bus voltage." as F2
    
    rectangle "Bit 00: SD Bus Power (RW)\n\n1 = Power on\n0 = Power off\n\nBefore setting, set SD Bus Voltage Select first.\nIf No Card state detected, bit shall be cleared.\nWhen cleared, stop driving CMD and DAT[3:0]\n(tri-state) and drive SDCLK to low level." as F3
}

F1 -[hidden]down-> F2
F2 -[hidden]down-> F3

note bottom
**Implementation Notes:**

1. Host Driver supplies SD Bus voltage by SD Bus Power,
   according to SD card OCR and supply voltage capabilities.
   If unsupported voltage selected, Host Controller may
   ignore writes and keep value at zero.

2. Host System shall not supply SD Bus power when
   SD Bus Power is set to 0 and can supply SD Bus power
   when set to 1 depending on system conditions
   (ex. Left of the battery).
end note

@enduml